In this lesson, we'll look at testing and debugging algorithms. An algorithm isn't useful if it has bugs, so testing means running it on inputs you already know the answer to, and debugging means finding and fixing things when the output comes out wrong. So what makes a good test case? A typical input is a regular case, like sorting a short list. A boundary case sits right at the edge of what the algorithm should handle, like an empty list or a list with just one item. And an edge case is an unusual but still valid input, like a list full of duplicates. Now, the usual suspects when it comes to bugs. There's the off-by-one, where a loop runs one time too many, or one too few. There's a wrong operator, like using less than instead of less than or equal to. A variable might be misinitialised, starting from the wrong value. Or steps might run in the wrong order when the order actually matters. The fix-and-retest loop looks like this. Run your algorithm. If the output is wrong, find the very first line that goes wrong, fix it, then re-test, including the cases that already passed, to make sure your fix didn't quietly break something else. Quick warning. A test that only passes on nice inputs isn't really a test, so push your algorithm with empty lists, single items, duplicates, and big inputs. Now let's see it in action.